WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.560
Yesterday, I walked the aisles of Target

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:05.920
looking for billion-dollar business

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.760
ideas. And I found seven boring aisles

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.400
just waiting to be disrupted. And I used

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.080
this guy's strategy to find them. The

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.240
same guy built this, and this, and this,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.960
turning three of the most boring

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.880
products you can think of, dish soap,

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.840
bandages, and vitamins, into three huge

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.960
exits, combining for over a billion

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.800
dollars. He did the same strategy three

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.760
separate times. First, he built Method

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.600
soap, sold to SC Johnson. He did the

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.200
same thing with Olly vitamins. Olly

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.160
vitamins did a hundred million dollars

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.640
in sales in just the first two years and

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.800
sold to Unilever. And he did it again

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.760
with Welly bandages, also sold that

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.520
company to Unilever. His name is Eric

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.160
Ryan, and he has a repeatable formula

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.160
for finding these opportunities. He's

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.000
done it three times already. So, today,

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.880
I'm going to use his formula and walk

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.640
through seven aisles in Target that are

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:49.640
just waiting to be disrupted. And one of

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.280
these aisles is a fifteen-billion-dollar

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.800
market that hasn't been changed for over

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.440
twenty years. Before I show you the

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.760
aisles, let me show you the formula, so

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.360
you understand how he finds these

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.360
opportunities. Here's how it works. So,

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.200
step one, Eric walks into a store, and

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.240
he looks for what he calls a sea of

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.519
sameness, an aisle where every single

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.240
product looks identical. Same packaging,

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:10.000
same colors, same design, same claims.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.840
His line is, when he sees that white

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.440
noise, he knows there's an opportunity.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.880
Because when consumers can't tell the

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.200
brands apart, there's no loyalty, just

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.040
habit. And habit is easy to break. So,

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.040
step two, after he finds the boring

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.800
opportunity, the aisle of sameness, he

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.480
finds the trend the big brands are

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.960
sleeping on. There's always one, he

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:30.360
says. Sustainability, wellness,

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.960
packaging simplification. The big brands

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.440
are too slow and too comfortable in

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.960
these boring categories to respond. Step

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:39.680
three, turn a have-to-buy into a

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.920
want-to-buy. Nobody enjoys buying soap,

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.600
gets excited about Band-Aids. His whole

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.240
move is making the product into

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.200
something that people actually want to

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.440
buy, that they get excited to buy. And

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.920
his one rule, change just one thing.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.760
Don't try to optimize every aspect of

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.720
the product, change one thing. With

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:58.400
Method soap, he just changed the

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.160
packaging, just changed the bottle and

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.000
it stood out on the shelf. With Welly

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.680
Bandages, he made them colorful and in a

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.000
cool package. With Ollie, again, he just

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.720
changed the packaging. Simple and easy

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.039
to understand. All right, so now that

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.600
you understand how to look for these

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:12.920
opportunities, let's jump in and I'm

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.520
going to show you the seven products I

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.959
found when I walked through Target that

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.440
I think are just waiting to be

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:19.320
disrupted. Now, real quick, none of the

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.120
opportunities in this video are a

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:23.160
guarantee. These are my opinions. This

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.760
certainly doesn't guarantee success just

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.560
by starting one of these brands. Isle

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:30.080
number one of seven, trash bags. Look at

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.840
this isle. Hefty, white bags, cardboard

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.840
box. Glad, white bags, cardboard box.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.600
Can you see the difference? Because I

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.720
can't. They're all the same. Trash bags

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.680
haven't changed since the 1960s. It's a

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.880
three and a half billion dollar category

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.720
with zero brand differentiation. Nobody

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.959
has loyalty to their trash bags. You

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.720
just grab whatever's there. There's a

00:02:44.959 --> 00:02:48.720
couple trends they're missing. One,

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.480
sustainability. People feel guilty about

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.320
single-use plastics, but the shelf

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.040
really gives them no alternatives.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.120
Another one is design. All of the trash

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.959
bags are sitting in cardboard boxes,

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.760
white bags. We've upgraded just about

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:02.120
every product in our kitchen except for

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.360
our trash bags. A startup called Hold On

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:06.000
made clean, compostable bags, modern

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:09.600
branding. Their first production run

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.920
sold out in 72 hours. 72 hours for trash

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.720
bags. That tells you everything you need

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.200
to know about the demand hiding in this

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.040
boring isle. Now, isle two is batteries.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:20.000
Duracell has looked like this since

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.040
1980. Energizer since 1982. We live in a

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.720
world of AI and electric vehicles, yet

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.519
the double A battery isle looks like

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.840
it's stuck in the 1980s. There's two

00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:28.800
trends I think are being missed here.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.600
First, rechargeable batteries should be

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.640
the default by now, but no one's made

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.680
that rechargeable switch feel modern and

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.600
premium. Second, batteries sit on

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.600
counters, in drawers, in your kitchen,

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.680
and are seen. Nobody's applied the

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:42.360
Method logic to the batteries. Nobody's

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:44.080
come up with any new designs. They're

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.040
all in the same kinds of packaging.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.160
There's no major challenger brand in

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:50.560
this category. None. No major DTC

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.720
brands, no design forward startups. This

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.680
might be the single most untouched

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.960
consumer product section in the store.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:59.160
Aisle number three, toilet paper. Look

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.280
at this aisle. Charmin, Cottonelle,

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:04.040
Target's in-house brand, they all look

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.280
exactly the same. It's a wall of white.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:09.120
Every brand competes on the same two

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.800
things, softness and ply count. That's

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.160
it. That's been the entire conversation

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.680
for decades. There's two trends that I

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.040
think can disrupt the toilet paper aisle

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.160
here. One is just design, making

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:19.840
something innovative, modern, unique,

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.440
it's different than the wall of white

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:23.360
that you see in there. And the other is

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:25.120
sustainability. And here's one thing

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.720
nobody talks about. Traditional toilet

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.800
paper is one of the biggest drivers of

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.280
old growth forest destruction in North

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:34.240
America. The NRDC grades all the major

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.280
toilet brands every year. Charmin,

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.960
Kirkland, most of the brands get failing

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.880
grades on sustainability in the toilet

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:41.480
paper aisle. At the same time, consumers

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.800
today are willing to pay more for

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:45.480
sustainable brands. Startups are already

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.320
proving the demand here. Who Gives A

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.960
Crap has already built a pretty

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:51.000
cult-like following with their

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.440
individually colorful wrapped toilet

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.360
paper rolls. Real Paper is bamboo-based,

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.360
going after the sustainability angle.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.120
They raised $14 million and are in some

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.440
of the Targets already. But there's no

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:04.360
dominant challenger, no dominant brand,

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.360
and the aisle is still 90% old stuff.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:08.040
There is huge opportunity for brands to

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:10.040
come in and disrupt the toilet paper

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.240
aisle on design, sustainability. Aisle

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:14.240
number four is light bulbs. Look at the

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.320
wall and try to figure out what to buy.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:19.160
What's 2,700 K? What's 800 lm?

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:21.680
What's a CRI rating? The average

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:23.600
consumer doesn't know and really doesn't

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.000
care. This is a master class in what

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:28.560
Eric Ryan calls over complication. When

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.560
a category takes itself so seriously

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.080
that it confuses its own customers.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.600
That's not a problem, that's an

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.880
opportunity. This is the same

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.400
opportunity that Eric Ryan saw with OLLY

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.360
vitamins. Vitamin aisle was so

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.760
complicated, he came out with a very

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.960
simple product that told the people

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:45.440
exactly what the vitamin did. Sleep,

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.600
anxiety, solutions were put right on the

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.360
bottle. I think there's a massive trend

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.320
that a light bulb aisle is sleeping on,

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:53.280
wellness lighting. Different types of

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:55.160
light can affect your mood, your sleep,

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.080
your energy. Expensive brands already

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.840
sell a hundred plus dollar light bulbs

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:00.600
to wealthy people looking for benefits

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:03.040
from specific types of light. But on the

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:05.520
shelf at Target, nothing exists. Imagine

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:08.280
a brand that just says morning energy,

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.120
evening calm, deep focus. Sell the

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:12.320
benefit, not the specs. Tell the

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:14.040
consumer what they're going to get out

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:15.920
of the light bulb. That's exactly what

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:18.040
Eric did with Olly vitamins. He didn't

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:20.880
sell melatonin, he sold sleep. He didn't

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:22.840
sell biotin, he sold beauty. Same exact

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.240
playbook, just a different aisle. Now,

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:26.800
aisle number five is the pest control

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.720
aisle. Look at this. Just about every

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.520
brand on this shelf looks like a

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.680
chemical weapon. Dead insects on

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:34.480
packaging, toxic warnings, dark

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:36.640
aggressive colors. This is an aisle that

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.760
perfectly proves Eric's point of if the

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:40.520
category takes itself too seriously,

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.480
it's probably hiding something. And this

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:44.000
category is hiding something. It's all

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.680
poison. Parents don't want this near

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:47.480
where their kids play. Pet owners don't

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:49.480
want this by their animals, and nobody

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.360
wants a picture of a dead cockroach on

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:53.120
their kitchen counter. Procter & Gamble

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.000
already kind of proved that this

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:56.800
category is looking for some innovation.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.560
They came up with a brand called Zevo,

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:00.919
pest control with plant-based

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.960
ingredients. No scary bugs on the label,

00:07:00.919 --> 00:07:05.040
safe around kids and pets. It became the

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:07.360
fastest growing insecticide brand in

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:09.480
America. But it's just one brand. The

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:11.360
rest of the shelf is still terrifying.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.360
There is huge opportunity for more

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:15.600
brands to come in and completely disrupt

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.840
this category. Aisle six, air

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.880
fresheners. A fifteen billion dollar

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:23.520
category that still smells like a mall

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:25.240
kiosk from 2003. Glade, Febreze, Air

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.160
Wick, they all compete on the same

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.520
thing, synthetic fragrances that

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:30.919
eliminate odors. The packaging is gaudy,

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.160
the scents are artificial, and the

00:07:30.919 --> 00:07:35.200
positioning hasn't changed in 20 years.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:37.360
The missed trend is wellness and clean

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:40.360
ingredients here. The mass retail shelf

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.760
at Target is still all synthetic aerosol

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:45.400
spray cans. Whatever brand becomes the

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:47.680
method of the fragrance aisle is going

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:49.720
to own that category. And lastly, aisle

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:52.240
seven, the food storage aisle, more

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:54.320
specifically, aluminum foil, cling wrap.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.840
Look at the shelf. Square cardboard

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:59.560
boxes with tear-off cling wrap, tear-off

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:02.200
aluminum foil, same delivery mechanism,

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:04.520
same style, same design. Haven't changed

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:06.120
in years. I have these in my drawers and

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:08.720
they constantly are getting ripped and

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:10.520
torn. This category is just waiting for

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.520
disruption. Meanwhile, we're living

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:14.640
through the biggest home organization

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:16.919
trend in history. The Home Edit, Marie

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:18.560
Kondo. People pay real money for things

00:08:16.919 --> 00:08:20.600
that look beautiful in their kitchen and

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:22.919
in their fridge. And this aisle is all

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:24.320
the same boring designs, waiting for

00:08:22.919 --> 00:08:26.360
disruption, waiting for something

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:28.240
different, unique, innovative, a

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:29.960
different design to come in and really

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.840
stand out. Here's the thing that most

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:33.599
people miss about what Eric Ryan does

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:35.159
with this strategy. He didn't invent

00:08:33.599 --> 00:08:36.680
anything. He didn't invent soap, he

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:38.440
didn't invent Band-Aids, he didn't

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:40.080
invent vitamins. He just looked at

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:42.000
shelves full of products that hadn't

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.400
changed in decades and asked, "What if

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:47.240
someone actually tried?" His formula in

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.600
one sentence: Find the sameness, find

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:52.760
the shift, and change one thing, and

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:54.680
turn a have-to-buy into a want-to-buy.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:56.720
And he proved you don't need a ton of

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:59.720
money to do it. Method started with just

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:02.080
$90,000. Ollie launched through a single

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:04.360
retailer. Welly was a small team with

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:06.200
one single insight: Bandages should be

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:07.920
more fun. He's still doing this right

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:10.320
now, by the way. He just launched a

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:11.880
functional candy brand called Candy and

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.920
a pain relief brand with Serena

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:15.720
Williams, called Will Perform. The guy

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:17.560
just won't stop finding these boring

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:19.760
shelves and making them interesting.

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:22.400
Every single one of these seven shelves

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:24.760
has a multi-billion-dollar opportunity

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:26.360
just waiting to be uncovered. Most of

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:29.080
these are multi-billion-dollar

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:31.120
categories run by major brands who gave

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:33.360
up on innovation decades ago. So, there

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:35.080
you have it. Seven aisles that I found

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:36.640
that I think have billion-dollar

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:38.440
opportunities in each one of them.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.200
Again, this isn't a guarantee. These are

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:41.960
just aisles that I found that I think

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:43.360
have huge opportunities. These are my

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:44.720
opinions. Just because you start one of

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:46.320
these doesn't mean it's going to be

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:48.120
successful. Real quick, I want to give a

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.200
shout-out to my oldest daughter, Ella.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:52.000
She filmed all target scenes, all of the

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:53.680
aisles, helped me out with the video. If

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:55.000
